On the FM-1 the panel's chip select is PC7, active low (HARDWARE-SPEC
HW-115). Upstream fed every SPI1 byte, single (BUF) and by DMA (CNT), to
the panel model whatever PC7 was, so a firmware that left PC7 high drew
in the emulator and nothing on a unit (the owner's report of the first
hardware build, start1, 2026-11-18). With the patch the FM-1's panel
ignores SPI1's bytes while PC7 is an output (`JL_PORTC->DIR` bit 7
clear) driven high (`OUT` bit 7 set). A PC7 never driven high (`OUT`
bit 7 clear, as at reset in the model) still selects it, so a firmware
that never touches PC7 draws as before. The transfer itself still
completes and raises its interrupt: only the panel ignores the bytes.
Not modelled: a chip select rising in the middle of a command, which
the panel would take as the end of that command (its parameters and
pixels so far kept or dropped as the datasheet says); the SMK-37's chip
select. The crate test lcd_ignores_bytes_while_its_chip_select_is_high
fails without it.

